 ways  with knee surgery and the thing to  revolutionize knee surgery of course was  there  scope the problem we're having with new  surgeries now knee injuries particularly  in the NFL is the wear patterns we're  getting on the articular cartilage  surfaces which is an overuse wear  pattern they get them from years of  playing football and that's the big  problem now is trying to figure out how  do we fix these cartilage so called  cartilage lesions in the weight-bearing  surfaces of the knee joint  you know the ACL is still a problem  obviously but we're pretty consistent  with getting ACLs back we're not a  hundred percent though the problem with  ACL surgeries is eight nine ten years  later most of them have developed some  arthritic change related back to the  injury so it's not something that's you  like to have but it can't be a problem  is with wear patterns even when you've  done a successful ACL reconstruction do  you think you know it's always something  that we have to worry about in the knee  but we've come a lot further in the knee  and we have in the shoulder or even the  elbow do you think there will be a  pre-emptive surgery for athletes in the  future preventative not that I know of  my role is that's not anything we can  make better with surgery better than the  good Lord made it so I doubt if that's  ever going to come to pass the big  problem the big thing is trying to  figure out how do we prevent the  injuries to begin with with just basic  rule changes and and preventative  exercises and not playing one sport and  specializing in a sport well months out  of the year  where you stand on little leaguers in  you know how they throw how often they  throw because you I'm sure I've seen a  lot of these elbow injuries and you've  seen them with now younger and younger  children so a lot of parents listening  to this program is there some kind of  words of advice from you know one of the  great surgeons in history that could  help us well that's certainly a problem  that I see daily I did three times  John's yesterday on either freshman in  college our young high school  this morning I've been seeing new  patients and so far I've had three rooms  I've been to seeing new patients they're  all 15 16 17 years of age that are  playing year-round baseball and it  injured their Tommy John's ligament so  the big thing for parents is they  anybody doing an overhead throwing  sports should have at least two months  off preferably three to four months off  each year when they're not doing an  overhead sport which means it shouldn't  specialize in play twelve months out of  the year but then one thing I can tell  them if they if they're playing and  they're playing with fatigue in youth  baseball meaning they have to take  seasonal fatigue or year-round fatigue  if they're doing that there's a 36 to  one times  that's a 3,600 percent increased chance  of injuring the shoulder or their elbow  play them youth baseball so fatigue is  the thing we have to prevent it so how  do we do that that's the big question I  know you're busy  I always appreciate your time and thank  you doctor we appreciate you and  especially with that piece of advice  there for parents of kids who are  playing high score youth baseball thank  you doctor well well thank you for  getting the message out and good luck to  all these football players this year  Thank You doc that's dr. James Andrews  found a partner medical director for the  Andrews Institute Gulf Breeze Florida  the Dan Patrick show weekday mornings on  audience
 

